Analysis: So by doing this command we are able to see the different sections in each of the programs. The first two programs again are separate programs and the last one is a combination of the first two that is also executable, the difference is that the frist two programs didn’t show memory addresses exactly while the last one, which is already linked and ready to be executed has memory addresses so that everything goes smoothly. For the first two files we have 12 and 13 sections but for the alst one we have 31 sections, this is because the linker added some extra sections and used all the sections from the already existing 12 and 13 sections. The relocatable files contain essential sections such as code, data, and symbol sections while the final executable merges these and gives them addresses to make the execution possible, it contains sections unique to it such as the startup/cleanup routines and so on.

•	What symbols are defined in each file?
Math_utils has the square function and demands nothing, so it works on its own.
Main has main and demands square and printf
And finally executable (square_prog) has everything because it’s already linked fine but demands printf because printf is obtained once it’s needed and not at the compiletime as the others are.

•	How the sections of the object files differ from the final executable?
Basically the object files include the core sections with no address assigned to them and also include the relocation tables for the data that is missing and need to be included yet, on the other hand the executable has allocated memory addresses and merges the sections and doesn’t have the relocation table because it contains all the necessary information. Also the executable has the startup and cleanup sections and several more sections intruduced by the linker.
•	The role of the linking process in combining the object files.
The linking file gives the differing programs the ability to know each other and run while being written in comepltely different files initially, that is, I as a programmer never write the printf logic, I merely use it while the linker is the one combining my program and the printf logic together giving me the freedom of writing different programs in different files and never think how they will communicate with each other.


